text:
Maria: Quick one — I rotated an org-level API key this morning and our Node sample app kept getting 401s on streaming (server-sent events) connections while non-streaming generation calls were fine. Anyone seen this?

Kevin: Hmm, streaming only? Did the sample app use an in-memory client or recreate per-request?

Maria: In-memory client (created at startup). Processes that reconnect (like HTTP) worked after a short time, but long-lived SSE streams failed until I restarted the process.

Samir: That sounds like the SDK is reading env vars at init and never reloading creds. Which env var did you rotate? REDWOOD_API_KEY or a project-scoped one?

Maria: REDWOOD_API_KEY (org-scoped). The new key has slightly different scopes (we tightened realtime scope during rollout). The streaming failure was a 401 with body: `{"error":"insufficient_scope","required":"realtime:connect"}` — non-stream requests had the scopes they needed.

Julie: Are we sure it's not a routing/caching layer? Could the gateway be sticky to old creds?

Maria: I dumped outgoing headers from the live process: it was still sending the old key that had been rotated out. So it's definitely the client still using the old token.

Kevin: Repro steps useful — thanks. Node SDK creates a client from process.env if you call `new Redwood({})` without credentials. If you pass `apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY` and don't re-read it, that value is frozen. Python's SDK has a `CredentialProvider` hook we added last year that can be a callback; Node didn't get that parity yet.

Samir: Right — parity gap. Short-term workaround: call a small function that reads process.env before creating the stream, or add a tiny reconnect wrapper that refreshes credentials on 401. Example (Node):

```js
async function openStream() {
  const apiKey = process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY;
  const client = new Redwood({ apiKey });
  const stream = await client.startStream(...);
  stream.on('error', async err => {
    if (err.status === 401) {
      // re-create client with fresh env var and reconnect
      await wait(200);
      await openStream();
    }
  });
}
openStream();
```

Julie: That's helpful as a stopgap. But for containers / k8s we can't keep restarting pods. We need the SDK to support rotated keys (and prefer explicit creds passed in) and also an env-var provider that can be swapped out.

Maria: Agreed. Also noticed inconsistency in env var precedence between CLI/sampleapps: some places use `REDWOOD_API_KEY`, others `REDWOOD_TOKEN`, and a couple of ancient samples refer to `RLW_API_KEY`. That makes rotations and onboarding confusing.

Kevin: Oof. We should standardize. Proposal: SDK precedence order: 1) explicit apiKey passed to client constructor, 2) `REDWOOD_API_KEY`, 3) `REDWOOD_PROJECT_KEY`, 4) `REDWOOD_ORG_KEY`, 5) CLI interactive/credential helper. And document it.

Samir: I can take a PR to add a `credentialProvider: () => Promise<string>` option to Node SDK so apps can inject refresh logic (same pattern as Python). Also add an internal fallthrough that reads env on every new request if a provider isn't supplied.

Julie: Please also add a short note to the onboarding sampleapp readme about rotation: show how to replace keys without downtime (use provider callback or graceful reconnect). New hires keep hitting this when they test streaming.

devex-bot: Created issue RED-3421: "Node SDK: credential provider + env-var precedence + sampleapp docs for rotation" and linked PR template.

Kevin: For immediate mitigation, I'll add a tweak in the hosted-sampleapp docker entrypoint that watches `/run/secrets/redwood_api_key` and, on change, sends SIGHUP to the process so it re-reads env and recreates clients. Not ideal but buys time.

Maria: Thanks — that would help teams using secrets mounts. Long term we need the SDK parity + docs. Can we also add a small checklist in the console UI when rotating org/project keys reminding owners to verify scopes (realtime, embeddings, etc.)? That would have prevented the missing `realtime:connect` scope.

Samir: Good call. I'll file a separate product ticket for a rotation checklist.

Julie: TL;DR action items: Samir -> SDK provider PR for Node, Kevin -> entrypoint hot-reload as bandaid, DevEx -> add sampleapp doc blurb + onboarding note, Product -> rotation checklist in console. I'll add these to the onboarding playbook. :+1:

Maria: Thanks team — leaving notes in the sampleapp repo and I'll test Samir's provider PR once it's up. :thumbsup:

Kevin: Also, small DX ask: can we standardize env var names in all examples and run a quick grep to find ancient `RLW_*` references? I can run that and open a cleanup PR.

Samir: I'll queue the Node SDK branch after the sprint freeze. Expect initial PR by EOW. :rocket:
